Add a Wintun TUN device so libipsec has a datapath on Windows.

Windows' own IPsec engine (kernel-wfp) drops UDP-encapsulated ESP in tunnel mode
with STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR, which upstream documents with no known workaround.
Behind NAT, UDP encapsulation is not optional, so a road-warrior client cannot
use it and has to do ESP in userland (libipsec) over a virtual adapter instead.
libipsec needs a tun_device_t, and there is none on Windows.

The plugin scaffolding -- FEATURE_TUN_DEVICE and the lib->tun registry, so a
plugin can supply the device that kernel-libipsec and kernel-pfroute create --
is taken from upstream's `wintun` branch (Andreas Steffen, 2023). That branch's
device itself is not usable: it has never been compiled (init_wintun() is not
valid C) and its read_packet()/write_packet()/up()/set_address() are empty stubs
returning TRUE. The device here is a real implementation:

  * The adapter is created via wintun.dll, which carries its own signed driver,
    so there is nothing to install. Wintun is layer 3, so unlike TAP there is no
    Ethernet or ARP emulation to write.

  * get_name() returns the adapter's GUID, because that is how kernel-iph names
    interfaces (AdapterName from GetAdaptersAddresses) -- and charon looks the
    device up by name to install the virtual IP and the routes on it.

  * Wintun signals readable packets with a Win32 event, but libipsec's packet
    router poll()s a file descriptor. A reader thread bridges the two: it waits
    on the event, drains the receive ring, and writes each packet length-prefixed
    into a loopback socketpair whose other end is the device's fd.

Two bugs in the upstream scaffolding are fixed as well: plugin_feature_load() was
missing a break, so FEATURE_RESOLVER fell through and every resolver plugin also
registered itself as a TUN device constructor; and the plugin now links iphlpapi,
which it needs to set the adapter's MTU.

The device also has to be registered from the plugin's constructor, not only via
its TUN_DEVICE feature: the loader runs every plugin's constructor before it
loads any features, and kernel-libipsec creates its TUN device inside its own
constructor.

